{"kind":"request","text":"ox-checker: audit the workroom UI for vocabulary consistency between what a person sees and what the durable record says, and report the findings. Hugh asked for this after noticing a tooltip reading \"disagree\" leading to an act the record calls \"dissent\", and I confirmed his instance at source before filing: ui/src/components/Toolbar.tsx routes three different labels — \"deny\" (line 115), \"disagree\" (line 122), and \"needs work\" (line 128) — all to the dissent action, and none of the three uses the word dissent, the word the filed record, the projection, and the documentation then show the user. The audit: walk the UI surfaces (labels, tooltips, buttons, empty states, headings, confirmations) in ui/src against the governed vocabulary the fold and docs use — request, promise, report, artifact, propose, ratify, dissent, supersede, assert, and the lifecycle words — and list every place the interface teaches a different word than the record it creates or displays. For each, name the file and line, the shown word, the record word, and whether the divergence is plausibly deliberate softening or plain drift. This is a survey producing findings, not a fix: deliver the findings as an assert resting on this request, ranked by how badly the mismatch would mislead someone learning the system from the UI. Repairs become follow-up implementation requests once hugh has seen the list, since some of these — the example included — may be deliberate voice choices that hugh wants kept.","body":{"area":"ui","conditions":"A findings assert resting on this request listing every UI-versus-record vocabulary divergence in ui/src at then-current main, each with file and line, the displayed word, the durable word, and a deliberate-or-drift judgment, ranked by potential to mislead; confirmed instances only, each verified at source; no code changes in this task.","severity":"low","to":"663e99d1a7876f383b8430669cfcd5633512bd45e545c790c65b60b87adc994a"}}